On 2015-01-07 13:55, Kyle Gates wrote:
> What issues would arise if ssd mode is activated because of a block lay=
er setting the rotational flag to zero? This happens for me running btrfs=
 on bcache. Would it be beneficial to pass the no_ssd flag?
> Thanks,
> Kyle
In theory, it would result in a greater degree of high-level=20
fragmentation.  You should (in theory) get marginally better performance =

by using no_ssd (or using a udev rule to set rotational to 1, as the=20
flag is writable).

Ideally, bcache shouldn't set the rotational flag to 0 unless the=20
backing store (the big device that the cache is reading from) also has=20
it set.

Also, ideally, we should be looking at the type of block device, not=20
just whether the rotational flag is 0 or not, as there are a number of=20
cases where rotational is 0 that enabling ssd will make things preform=20
worse (Xen/Virtio block devices come immediately to mind).


